# chains — reasoning chains: premises jointly imply conclusion. See README.md.
{"conclusion": "n_b3fab74520", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_568c8ff385", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_318a62435d", "n_b504ac8f2b"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Add-1 Smoothing.md", "message": "Why It's Needed", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because unsmoothed bigram models give unseen bigrams probability zero and NLL of a zero-probability token is +∞, the loss is only finite and trainable if every bigram probability is made non-zero, which is what add-1 smoothing does."}
{"conclusion": "n_823bd5e47d", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_1b3ad47091", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_83c18bba5f", "n_dd570ef650"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Adam (adaptive moments).md", "message": "The equations (Kingma & Ba, 2015) + Why bias correction", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Substituting the bias-corrected moments m̂ ≈ g and v̂ ≈ g^2 into Adam's update rule makes the step magnitude approximately η·sign(g), i.e. scale-invariant sign descent at a fixed learning rate."}
{"conclusion": "n_202d966ca9", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_f8cfffdb10", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_3f2656eb18", "n_5e131ccb54"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Acutest Unit Testing.md", "message": "Why TEST_ASSERT's \"dire\" consequences are acceptable", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "TEST_ASSERT aborts mid-test, and since Acutest runs each test in its own isolated child process whose resources the OS reclaims over short-lived fixtures, that destructive abort is acceptable in practice."}
{"conclusion": "n_d8495c4773", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_83da88b195", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_0a3689f11d", "n_35ff145855"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering Roadmap v2.md", "message": "\"Why decode is memory-bandwidth-bound (read all 2P bytes of weights per token → bound below the 208:1 FLOPS/bandwidth ratio)\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because decoding re-reads all 2P weight bytes per token while the hardware's FLOPS-to-bandwidth balance is 208:1, transformer decoding is memory-bandwidth-bound."}
{"conclusion": "n_a68687f3ec", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_d1f5b51631", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_8202bf9a64"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA).md", "message": "What AAA Gives You: \"the test runner stops at the first failed assertion, so bundling many independent checks into one test destroys diagnostic precision\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because a test runner stops at the first failed assertion, bundling many independent checks into one test destroys diagnostic precision."}
{"conclusion": "n_64dafd13dc", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_145a97c53e", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_12b96f2ac7", "n_bb6cd910a5"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Automatic differentiation.md", "message": "Core idea and Two modes, Forward mode", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Chaining local derivative rules while propagating sensitivities forward in a left-to-right sweep entails that seeding the input tangent with a direction vector r yields the Jacobian-vector product J_f(x)r, i.e. the directional derivative."}
{"conclusion": "n_3dfa6996a3", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_be4cb5bf14", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_4080ab53bf", "n_8630af0c26"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "Derivation (from Conditional Probability)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Equating the two expressions for the shared joint probability in the definition of conditional probability yields Bayes' theorem P(A|B) = P(B|A)·P(A)/P(B)."}
{"conclusion": "n_60d06b9907", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_e942a7d39e", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_4080ab53bf", "n_8630af0c26"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "No independence assumption is needed — the derivation only equates two expressions for the same joint probability.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since the derivation uses only the conditional-probability definition and the equality of two expressions for the same joint probability P(A∩B), no independence assumption enters Bayes' theorem."}
{"conclusion": "n_3a5c28c79a", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_77e8df60c0", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_5c2bfc8cfc", "n_888f119ead"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "Disease Test Example — Intuitive", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Base Rate Fallacy.md", "message": "The 3-Step Method (concrete population)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Applying the concrete-population method to a 1-in-1000 disease with a 99%-sensitive, 1%-false-positive test gives 11 positives of whom 1 is sick, hence a posterior near 9%."}
{"conclusion": "n_0ad7579ac2", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_2d49da2539", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_96cf708f7d", "n_d48a98c370"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Chain Rule Decomposition.md", "message": "# Chain Rule Decomposition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Chain Rule for Neural Networks.md", "message": "## Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the chain rule writes a composed derivative as a product of local derivatives and a multi-layer network's loss gradient is exactly such a product along the layer path, backpropagation is the chain rule applied repeatedly from output to input."}
{"conclusion": "n_08e5617a65", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_06d836dc21", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_183d4bd75b", "n_410ba29728"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Integer Mechanics (Underflow & Type Promotion).md", "message": "### The subtle bug in `(used_kb / memory.total_kb) * 100`", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since mixing an unsigned long with a double promotes it to double and integer division would truncate the fraction to zero, leading with 100.0 keeps the division floating-point and yields the correct percentage."}
{"conclusion": "n_2fe5a86977", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "arg_cf91c501e2", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_06133d18f1", "n_da99b036c7"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bytecode.md", "message": "## Why bytecode?", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because bytecode is architecture-independent and a virtual machine executes it, the same bytecode runs on any device equipped with the right VM."}
{"conclusion": "n_d068e86316", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "arg_24b42d6013", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_5948b9d37c"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Compiler.md", "message": "An interpreter, executing line-by-line, simply doesn't have time for this analysis — it trades optimization for instant feedback.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because optimization needs the whole-program view that a compiler has, an interpreter running line by line has no time for that analysis and must trade optimization for instant feedback."}
{"conclusion": "n_97b6276b36", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "arg_5a7cdcf175", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_69cc6762f5", "n_b367774b99"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Conditional Independence.md", "message": "This assumption is usually false in real data, but Naive Bayes classifiers still perform well because they only need to rank classes correctly, not output calibrated probabilities.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since the conditional-independence assumption is usually false but Naive Bayes only needs correct class ranking rather than calibrated probabilities, the classifier can still perform well."}
{"conclusion": "n_aba3dd13ef", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "arg_8d417c522d", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_5db6cf7065", "n_d8f7531d60"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Conjugate Priors.md", "message": "Without conjugacy you need MCMC sampling or variational inference to approximate the posterior. With conjugacy you update two numbers.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because a Beta prior and a Beta posterior belong to the same family, the Beta-Binomial update reduces to updating two numbers instead of approximating the posterior by MCMC sampling or variational inference."}
{"conclusion": "n_2ccb0fb8bb", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "arg_2b8c675f9e", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_2aed8cc303", "n_cfb9489513"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Context-Free Grammar (CFG).md", "message": "Why Regular Languages Aren't Enough", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because a CFG production expands one non-terminal regardless of context, recursive rules can encode the unbounded nesting that fixed-state regular languages cannot track."}
{"conclusion": "n_b86ef17355", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "arg_9be2609a06", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_7d38e49d90", "n_b67fed0513"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Counts-to-Probabilities (keepdim trap).md", "message": "## The keepdim=True Trap", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Row normalization is the goal, but without keepdim the row sums broadcast as a row vector and divide columns, so correct row normalization requires keepdim=True and its (27, 1) shape"}
{"conclusion": "n_9b7cce5777", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "arg_6ca6d0fb91", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_3d450a852a"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cross-Entropy from NLL.md", "message": "every term but one vanishes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Evaluating cross-entropy under one-hot labels kills every term but the correct class, leaving −log(ŷ_correct), i.e. the NLL"}
{"conclusion": "n_e5c3a3640c", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "arg_76e4071bec", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_253bfdeea6"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cross-Entropy from NLL.md", "message": "## Floor rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "H(P,Q) = H(Q) + KL(Q∥P) together with KL ≥ 0 yields the floor H(P,Q) ≥ H(Q), tight exactly when P = Q"}
{"conclusion": "n_f8cefed4b8", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "arg_9e6905fc02", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_d4d3fd1ab3"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C String Buffer Boundaries.md", "message": "## The +1 Null-Terminator Bug", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A null-terminated string needs one byte beyond its characters, so a buffer sized exactly to the maximum column length overflows when a maximum-length value is inserted"}
{"conclusion": "n_e3e0df570a", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "arg_5fdd3fb10e", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_4d37ccc2fd"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Curse of Dimensionality.md", "message": "Distance concentration bullets and detector bullet", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because nearest and farthest neighbours become nearly equidistant in high dimensions, distance-based methods such as k-NN, k-means, and Euclidean clustering lose their discriminating power."}
{"conclusion": "n_afd6bf5fc9", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "arg_85adc9e3b0", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_6561fb0fb6"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Directional derivative.md", "message": "definition formula plus 'A partial derivative is just a directional derivative along a coordinate axis'", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Setting the direction r to a coordinate basis vector reduces the Jacobian-vector product J_f(x) r to the corresponding partial derivative."}
{"conclusion": "n_d1c7c7cc8b", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "arg_04bce9622f", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_8d27e2aab9"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Logits & Log-odds.md", "message": "Only differences matter — shift invariance: z + c·1 leaves p unchanged", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because softmax divides e^{z_c} by the sum of e^{z_j}, adding the same constant c to every logit multiplies numerator and denominator by e^c, so the probabilities — and hence the information — depend only on logit differences."}
{"conclusion": "n_1aec427e9c", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "arg_3c272f153b", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_8630c47d01"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Loss Functions (PyTorch).md", "message": "Common Pitfall", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "If nn.CrossEntropyLoss already applies softmax to raw logits internally, then also applying softmax in forward() feeds it probabilities instead of logits, applying softmax twice and preventing proper learning."}
{"conclusion": "n_6ea4682110", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "arg_028ee1def3", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_096c42c723"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Transformations.md", "message": "Stability. In recurrent networks and dynamical systems, eigenvalues with magnitude > 1 cause outputs to explode.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since a matrix only scales its eigenvector by λ, repeated application multiplies components along that direction by λ^n, so eigenvalues of magnitude above 1 explode and below 1 vanish — the vanishing/exploding gradient problem."}
{"conclusion": "n_600f2aa8e3", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "arg_518784dc97", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_fe63e7ad41"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Entropy (Average Surprise).md", "message": "The distribution feeds itself, so there is no second distribution to borrow weights from", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because in entropy the same distribution supplies both the surprise and the weight, entropy needs no second distribution, whereas cross-entropy must take weights from the true Q and surprises from the model P."}
{"conclusion": "n_3da194caea", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "arg_45463be725", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_f7866ac850"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Environment Variables (Shell).md", "message": "Why export/unset must be built-ins", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since a child program cannot modify its parent's environment, export and unset must be built-ins that mutate the shell's own environment rather than external programs."}
{"conclusion": "n_15155d3148", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "arg_c1403043c3", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_1f888f3065", "n_8b4be9e9e9"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Euler number e.md", "message": "Why it is special", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Exponential derivatives.md", "message": "This is the bridge to Euler number e", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Applying d/dt a^t = ln(a)·a^t at a = e, where ln(e) = 1, gives d/dt e^t = e^t, so e is the base whose exponential is its own derivative."}
{"conclusion": "n_86c8aab20a", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "arg_241e14c371", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_0f93a34554", "n_9eb290ed01"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Forward-Mode Autodiff.md", "message": "**Cost:** O(n) passes for n input variables / **Why not for neural nets**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since forward-mode autodiff costs one forward pass per input and a neural network has millions of weight inputs, it would need millions of forward passes, so forward mode is computationally impossible for neural-network gradients."}
{"conclusion": "n_6bdca16fc9", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "arg_429f9c4e1c", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_46df78e952"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Functional programming.md", "message": "**Immutability** / \"Concurrency-safe (immutable data can be read by multiple threads simultaneously)\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because functional data is never mutated, it can be read by multiple threads at once, so functional programs are concurrency-safe and avoid shared-state concurrency bugs."}
{"conclusion": "n_e7886a266e", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "arg_e09cf38e57", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_9218903511"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gaussian distribution.md", "message": "## Conjugacy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Gaussian priors and Gaussian likelihoods are conjugate, so the posterior is Gaussian and Bayesian updates can often be computed in closed form."}
{"conclusion": "n_b8f72f72e1", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "arg_a41c292f40", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_a85a9ab7ed"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient Accumulation (+=).md", "message": "\"This is why all backward functions use `+=` instead of `=`\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the total gradient is the sum of every path's contribution, backward functions must add contributions with += instead of overwriting them with =."}
{"conclusion": "n_83f6cb85d7", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "arg_29803d7378", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_228f630ee9", "n_fcb46ed9ec"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/L2 Regularization as Smoothing.md", "message": "**The mechanism:** When `W = 0`, logits = 0, exp(0) = 1 for every class.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Zero weights make the softmax uniform and the L2 penalty pulls the weights toward zero, so L2 regularization acts as the neural counterpart of count smoothing."}
{"conclusion": "n_89b934da1d", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "arg_91ff2fca8a", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_c307d636d9", "n_c428cb4366", "n_d41b6ded34"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Label Smoothing.md", "message": "## The loss, regrouped", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the soft target is the mixture (1-eps)*one-hot + eps/K and cross-entropy against it decomposes into a hard-CE term plus a uniform term, the unattainable demand p_true = 1 is replaced by a demand of (1-eps) with a floor of eps/K, so a finite logit gap becomes optimal."}
{"conclusion": "n_b0cabf1d3d", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "arg_56fbcc41b2", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_82194746c9", "n_9af1987f46"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Laplace Smoothing.md", "message": "## Why It Works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Adding 1 to every count and V to the denominator keeps the word distribution summing to 1 while making every count strictly positive, so no unseen word can zero out the probability product."}
{"conclusion": "n_f6f3ea639a", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "arg_6a26103a5a", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_87986567e9", "n_a59d342c92"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian-vector product.md", "message": "If \\(r\\) is a coordinate basis vector, the JVP reduces to a partial derivative.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian matrix.md", "message": "What the entries mean", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the Jacobian's (i, j) entry is the partial derivative of output i with respect to input j, applying the Jacobian to a coordinate basis vector selects one column and yields a partial derivative."}
{"conclusion": "n_9db13a904e", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "arg_054e660f58", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_5a52d00ac7", "n_d990aee664"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Java Class Body Rules.md", "message": "Why This Matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Java Static Initializer Block.md", "message": "The Problem It Solves", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Static initialisation that needs .put() or .add() action statements cannot live in the open class body, since the class body admits only declarations, so Java needs a static {} block for complex static initialisation."}
{"conclusion": "n_a6b4219ddf", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "arg_687198447e", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_96391381b5", "n_b743657442"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities.md", "message": "Relationship to probability rules", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Dividing the joint count n_ij/N by the marginal P(X = x_i) = c_i/N gives P(Y | X) = n_ij/c_i, so the conditional probability divides by the conditioning column total."}
{"conclusion": "n_9c22b3f21e", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "arg_2d7cfcdc3d", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_af3b92dd1b"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Naive Bayes.md", "message": "The \"Naive\" Part + Formula", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Assuming features are conditionally independent given the class factorizes the class-conditional joint into a product of per-feature likelihoods, which is what makes the Naive Bayes score a prior times a product of likelihoods."}
{"conclusion": "n_7f8d348e75", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "arg_78df4ff1ea", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_8b78684d93"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Natural logarithm.md", "message": "\"That identity immediately gives the derivative rule\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Differentiating a^t = e^{ln(a)t} with respect to t yields d/dt a^t = ln(a)·a^t."}
{"conclusion": "n_4d0b3a8ed9", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "arg_869bc0fdc9", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_7b3cf6bb31"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Newtons law of cooling.md", "message": "\"Its solution is exponential decay\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A temperature difference whose rate of change is proportional to itself, with constant of proportionality −k, therefore decays exponentially as e^{−kt}."}
{"conclusion": "n_3dc0bc1046", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "arg_bd5db2f7e2", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_4cf72b10a2"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/One-Hot Encoding.md", "message": "Why not use the integer directly? + One-hot definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because integer category codes carry no ordinal meaning, encoding a category as a one-hot vector avoids implying an ordering that multiplying the integer by a weight would assert."}
{"conclusion": "n_0da37125f7", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "arg_31384ad750", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_b735af58c6"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient descent.md", "message": "If you ask for the direction delta that gives the largest decrease among small steps of fixed size, the answer points opposite the gradient", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The first-order Taylor model entails that, for a small step of fixed size, the direction of largest decrease is opposite the gradient."}
{"conclusion": "n_cda9785c79", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "arg_c2fd62de14", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_4ff16dde71", "n_aa710a9883"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hidden Layers Generalize via Shared Weights.md", "message": "the shared weights recognize the pattern", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Shared weights that encode general sub-pattern detectors and stay fixed at test time let the network recognize an unseen word from a familiar sub-pattern."}
{"conclusion": "n_a66bf3c964", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "arg_eba3596feb", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_274b248dfa"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Independence of random variables.md", "message": "Derivation from conditional probability", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Substituting P(Y|X) = P(X,Y)/P(X) into the conditional-equals-marginal condition and multiplying both sides by P(X) yields the factorization condition, so factorization is not a separate idea."}
{"conclusion": "n_6b3c09e68f", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "arg_0c9d0d074b", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_a625345078"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Interpreter.md", "message": "Because the interpreter reads code on the fly, it cannot look ahead", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An interpreter's inability to look ahead prevents whole-program optimization, which is why it is slower than a compiler."}
{"conclusion": "n_6084926989", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "arg_300274d88a", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_23fae3a858", "n_fd228c5f43"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Saddle Points (critical point triage).md", "message": "Why saddles dominate in high dimensions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Classifying a saddle as a mixed-sign critical point, and noting that a local minimum needs all d eigenvalue signs positive while a saddle needs only one to disagree, entails that a high-dimensional critical point is almost surely a saddle."}
{"conclusion": "n_facd897c29", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "arg_778a0752ae", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_8213e2f5fe", "n_f1c8353590"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sequential Bayesian Updating.md", "message": "Order Invariance", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because a conjugate posterior returns to the same family with the batch's counts added to its parameters, day-by-day updating and one batch update reach the identical Beta(13,9) posterior."}
{"conclusion": "n_b2b09ae50d", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "arg_c30d9994d9", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_1dcf2db449", "n_1e75c92f20"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shell Built-ins & Process Isolation.md", "message": "Built-ins vs External Programs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since shell state lives only in the shell process's memory and OS isolation prevents children from writing it, commands that must change that state must be built-ins while commands whose effects persist outside the process can be external."}
{"conclusion": "n_62ef509544", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "arg_bfa2c472f5", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_08ba208151", "n_372be4886f"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Short-circuit evaluation.md", "message": "Crucially, these operators return the **actual operand value**, not just `True`/`False`", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Defining A and B and A or B as each returning one of their operands entails that the operators yield actual operand values rather than booleans."}
{"conclusion": "n_bdf548788c", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_aa8296e33e", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_6fa883321d", "n_ffb3ef6255"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "PCA as an anomaly detector; Shape fit lives across, depth lives along", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Projecting onto the top-k axes and defining error as the dropped-coordinate energy together entail that PCA anomaly detection measures distance across the subspace only, so a point far out along a kept direction is never flagged."}
{"conclusion": "n_c852052d1a", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_97ecb0f953", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_639e8af0f6", "n_7a355a13f5"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA Sign Ambiguity (svd_flip).md", "message": "The practical caveat: sign-sensitive thresholds", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because eigenvectors are defined up to sign and a flip moves a direction together with its signed coordinates, any threshold on signed PCA scores is convention-dependent and must be recast in absolute value."}
{"conclusion": "n_3ecd17b1b7", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_c22145fb12", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_cb57bf958d"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PMF vs PDF.md", "message": "Key Distinction", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since a PDF reports density rather than point probability, the probability of any exact value of a continuous variable is zero and probability must come from integrating over an interval."}
{"conclusion": "n_001ff7cf16", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_c035e54cc4", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_70c9397e74"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Pooling (MaxPool2d).md", "message": "intro; Three Purposes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Keeping only the maximum of each 2×2 window makes the pooled output unchanged when an object shifts slightly within a window, which is the mechanism of pooling's translation invariance."}
{"conclusion": "n_4467d0fdf4", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_f418dcd8e5", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_32d548d1cb", "n_4c1742a108"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability & Distributions for ML.md", "message": "Conditional Probability & Bayes' Theorem", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability foundations.md", "message": "3. Basic probability rules", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The conditional probability definition plus the product-rule factorization P(A intersect B) = P(B|A)P(A) yields Bayes' theorem P(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A)/P(B)."}
{"conclusion": "n_3bf07f579a", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_4a61a5ab84", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_52f7d52255", "n_b32af5d412"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability mass and density functions.md", "message": "Why f(x) can exceed 1", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because interval probabilities come only from integrating the PDF and the sole constraints are nonnegativity and unit total area, a density's height is unconstrained and may exceed 1."}
{"conclusion": "n_af46d2e2f0", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_43d5ff1e37", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_2fa8fc5801", "n_c4a9f0454f"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability Mass Function (PMF).md", "message": "Every classifier output is a PMF.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability & Distributions for ML.md", "message": "Softmax & Cross-Entropy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Softmax produces nonnegative values that sum to 1, which is exactly a PMF, so a classifier's final softmax output is a probability mass function."}
{"conclusion": "n_96213c3bb2", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_e4f913bf88", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_7ba0e74c8a"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PyTorch Model Building.md", "message": "The output layer returns raw logits (no softmax) because nn.CrossEntropyLoss() applies softmax internally.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the loss applies softmax internally, the model must emit raw logits rather than probabilities."}
{"conclusion": "n_a801c8a695", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_b98786ea1d", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_6f6e540060", "n_9c98597cdd"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Random variable.md", "message": "Pre-image; Why formalize it this way", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because X maps outcomes into T and the pre-image of a subset of T is a set of outcomes in Ω, probability about values of X is the Ω-measure of those pre-images, so distributions on T are induced by the measure on Ω."}
{"conclusion": "n_9da4e53d0b", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_6bd8dea21c", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_f626cb404e"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Red-Green-Refactor.md", "message": "Why Red Must Come First", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Only an observed failure shows that a test runs the new code and that its assertion can fail, so a test never seen red may pass unconditionally on broken code and thus manufactures false confidence."}
{"conclusion": "n_e4ca963032", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_e242070e3a", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_3805f8fa19", "n_6946f75c10"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Reverse-Mode Autodiff & Backprop.md", "message": "Forward mode needs n passes; reverse mode needs m passes; Neural network asymmetry", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With forward mode needing n passes and reverse mode m passes, a neural network's millions of inputs against one output make one backward pass sufficient, so reverse mode is the only practical choice."}
{"conclusion": "n_4f3e94a88c", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_3221f11f78", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_13f72b9341", "n_716e4c1828"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Row-Select Property.md", "message": "What this means", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because a one-hot input times W selects a single row and softmax of that row is the conditional next-character distribution, the single-layer neural bigram fits the same 27×27 conditional table that counting estimates."}
{"conclusion": "n_0c50b19230", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "arg_ec9baa79d7", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_28cab3d2ca", "n_977f07b18d"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Word Embeddings (nn.Embedding).md", "message": "During training, backpropagation updates them so words appearing in similar contexts end up close together.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because embeddings are per-word lookup vectors updated by backpropagation, words occurring in similar contexts receive similar updates and end up close together."}
{"conclusion": "n_ef6ae2dea4", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "arg_72f9a89254", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_a6a9129207", "n_bebbf02c7b"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mini-batch Noise (two effects).md", "message": "Even where the true gradient is zero, the mini-batch estimate usually is not, and that random push moves the iterate off the saddle.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Full-batch GD has no direction to move when the true gradient is exactly zero, whereas the mini-batch estimate is almost never exactly zero, so the random push of mini-batch noise moves the iterate off the saddle."}
{"conclusion": "n_74fc84174e", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "arg_7e9187e67c", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_8cf5066237"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell.md", "message": "Implication: If cd were an external program, it would change its own working directory, then exit — leaving the shell's $PWD unchanged.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because a child process cannot write to the parent shell's memory and its changes vanish on exit, any command that must change the shell's own state has to be a built-in."}
{"conclusion": "n_3ac0d02845", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "arg_7ee5837ba7", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_512f0bf805", "n_6e920d9b31"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MLE vs MAP Estimation.md", "message": "With massive data the likelihood overwhelms any prior and MLE ≈ MAP", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "MLE maximizes the likelihood alone while MAP multiplies it by a fixed prior, so once massive data makes the likelihood dominate, the two estimates coincide."}
{"conclusion": "n_58f87fe530", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "arg_10a1f0f677", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_00daef568b", "n_76e63e86ca", "n_eb70c00030"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Multivariate Taylor series.md", "message": "Two especially important cases are T1 and T2", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "T_1 is a plane fitted from value and gradient and T_2 adds Hessian curvature, so gradient descent follows a local plane while Newton's method fits a local quadratic bowl."}
{"conclusion": "n_965b68822f", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "arg_e3d473ea60", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_586e041e1f", "n_ab6b5054c6"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Topological Sort for Backprop.md", "message": "Algorithm", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the backward pass requires every node to have all incoming gradient contributions before propagating, and post-order topological sorting orders inputs before outputs, iterating the reversed topological list gives the correct outputs-first gradient propagation order."}
{"conclusion": "n_b419fbd296", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "arg_0c6402f0c9", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_4eaafda1d3"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Total differential.md", "message": "Level sets", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since the total differential measures the first-order change of F, staying on a level set where F is constant forces dF = 0."}
{"conclusion": "n_2f853acb0f", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "arg_4e5ad401a3", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_55a1923e22"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transfer Learning (PyTorch).md", "message": "Why It Works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because early CNN layers learn universal visual features that transfer across domains, a model pre-trained on ImageNet can be fine-tuned on a small medical or agricultural dataset instead of being trained from scratch."}
{"conclusion": "n_0d75a31c7f", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "arg_26db8bb0bf", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_e9ba15c381", "n_fd24ce35dc"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Softmax Subtract-Max Trick.md", "message": "## The Problem / ## The Fix", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since exp overflows above roughly 709 in float64 and subtracting max(z) is algebraically identical to plain softmax, subtracting the max prevents overflow while leaving the output unchanged."}
{"conclusion": "n_508a994b74", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "arg_61e6d45bf1", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_320f2c6cc1", "n_fcbe56b2a3"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/sscanf %n & Line Advancement.md", "message": "## Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because %n counts only the characters the format has matched and %lu stops before the trailing unit, the format string must consume that unit (or lines must be advanced whole) for %n-based advancement to stay on line boundaries."}
{"conclusion": "n_2618eb311f", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "arg_e8fee0e0a8", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_87fe284b5e"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/SSH — Public-Key Auth & Remote Commands.md", "message": "The private key is **never transmitted**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because authentication is a challenge that the client signs and the server verifies with the public key, the private key never has to cross the network."}
{"conclusion": "n_de1ab5941f", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "arg_ef10431d65", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_5d24b14315", "n_f059d921bd"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Testable Seam.md", "message": "## The Pattern / ## Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Given that environment-dependent tests are flaky and the seam isolates pure parsing from the live fetch, substituting a fixture at that seam makes any failure attributable to the parser alone."}
{"conclusion": "n_af9c8ef5d1", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "arg_760c15ce96", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_74020eac13", "n_a84252bdcb"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variance is Non-Negative (PSD Covariance).md", "message": "The chain, steps 2–5", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since projected variance along any direction equals uᵀCu and that quadratic form is a non-negative squared norm, every eigenvalue of the covariance matrix is a non-negative variance, so a negative eigenvalue can only be numerical noise."}
{"conclusion": "n_31eac97346", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "arg_8a5067fcd3", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_941223e6d1", "n_b741eddea6"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variation of Information.md", "message": "\"Why the geometric flip survives\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Building VI from the joint entropy bookkeeping rather than from mutual information alone makes V(X,X) cancel identically to 0 for every entropy value, which is why a geometric flip of MI satisfies the identity axiom that arithmetic flips of I cannot."}
{"conclusion": "n_f6ccc93113", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "arg_81b1ea6f5a", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_d5efa8587c"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variation of Information.md", "message": "Why V is a metric — and MI is not / What the metric property buys", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The metric axioms of variation of information yield both the triangle-inequality bounds through a reference point and the zero-iff-relabelling guarantee used to compare clusterings."}
{"conclusion": "n_331546e5d1", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "arg_27d0076dcb", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_119081ddc2"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Waterbed Theory.md", "message": "Core idea / Key insight for design", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "If complexity has an irreducible minimum and only relocates when pushed, then the only available design objective is choosing where to place it, not removing it."}
