{"conclusion": "n_823bd5e47d", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:24:19Z", "id": "arg_d177923591", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_1e44bffa88", "n_83c18bba5f", "n_dd570ef650"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Adam (adaptive moments).md", "message": "The equations (Kingma & Ba, 2015) + Why bias correction", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Substituting the bias-corrected moments m̂ ≈ g and v̂ ≈ g^2 into Adam's update rule makes the step magnitude approximately η·sign(g), i.e. scale-invariant sign descent at a fixed learning rate."}
{"conclusion": "n_e5c3a3640c", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:24:19Z", "id": "arg_0a2eea8089", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_253bfdeea6", "n_8087d22e07"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cross-Entropy from NLL.md", "message": "## Floor rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "H(P,Q) = H(Q) + KL(Q∥P) together with KL ≥ 0 yields the floor H(P,Q) ≥ H(Q), tight exactly when P = Q"}
{"conclusion": "n_600f2aa8e3", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:24:19Z", "id": "arg_3244aa9f63", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_3d450a852a", "n_fe63e7ad41"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Entropy (Average Surprise).md", "message": "The distribution feeds itself, so there is no second distribution to borrow weights from", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because in entropy the same distribution supplies both the surprise and the weight, entropy needs no second distribution, whereas cross-entropy must take weights from the true Q and surprises from the model P."}
{"conclusion": "n_3da194caea", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:24:19Z", "id": "arg_fb8744ba47", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_74fc84174e", "n_f7866ac850"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Environment Variables (Shell).md", "message": "Why export/unset must be built-ins", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since a child program cannot modify its parent's environment, export and unset must be built-ins that mutate the shell's own environment rather than external programs."}
{"conclusion": "n_a66bf3c964", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:24:19Z", "id": "arg_316b432ab5", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_274b248dfa", "n_8630af0c26"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Independence of random variables.md", "message": "Derivation from conditional probability", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Substituting P(Y|X) = P(X,Y)/P(X) into the conditional-equals-marginal condition and multiplying both sides by P(X) yields the factorization condition, so factorization is not a separate idea."}
{"conclusion": "n_f6f3ea639a", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:24:19Z", "id": "arg_9b4eeea6e8", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_87986567e9", "n_9aff412bb9", "n_a59d342c92"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian-vector product.md", "message": "If \\(r\\) is a coordinate basis vector, the JVP reduces to a partial derivative.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian matrix.md", "message": "What the entries mean", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the Jacobian's (i, j) entry is the partial derivative of output i with respect to input j, applying the Jacobian to a coordinate basis vector selects one column and yields a partial derivative."}
{"conclusion": "n_9db13a904e", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:24:19Z", "id": "arg_83bfcd7ab1", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_5a52d00ac7", "n_80e62f4f2a", "n_d990aee664"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Java Class Body Rules.md", "message": "Why This Matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Java Static Initializer Block.md", "message": "The Problem It Solves", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Static initialisation that needs .put() or .add() action statements cannot live in the open class body, since the class body admits only declarations, so Java needs a static {} block for complex static initialisation."}
{"conclusion": "n_a6b4219ddf", "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:24:19Z", "id": "arg_a5efb6b785", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_2e9b5b3ff9", "n_74b9588749", "n_96391381b5", "n_b743657442"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities.md", "message": "Relationship to probability rules", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Dividing the joint count n_ij/N by the marginal P(X = x_i) = c_i/N gives P(Y | X) = n_ij/c_i, so the conditional probability divides by the conditioning column total."}
