{"conclusion": "n_b591a7427e", "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "arg_aa85652370", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_90deb2befe", "n_df070e92a2"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "Every ambiguity in your prompt is a branch point where the model guesses.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "at an unresolved fork the mass splits across all plausible candidate arms and the autoregressive loop samples from that split", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "A prompt only conditions the next-token distribution, and an autoregressive loop samples from that distribution token by token; therefore an unresolved choice in the prompt leaves several continuations live with the probability mass divided among them, and the decoder emits whichever one it samples."}
{"conclusion": "n_c74c812b0a", "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "arg_7a10885feb", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_b591a7427e"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "The specific version constrains the output space. Fewer valid outputs means higher probability of getting the one you want.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Because an unresolved choice leaves several arms live with the probability mass divided among them, an instruction that deletes arms leaves the same mass concentrated on fewer survivors, which is why fewer valid outputs means a higher probability of getting the desired one."}
{"conclusion": "n_a54e93e026", "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "arg_8dd90b8adf", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_4f86363788"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "The more specific the role, the narrower the distribution, the higher the quality. But there is a limit.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Because a role prompt works only by biasing sampling toward the expert region of the training data, a role so narrow that the region holds few high-quality examples leaves the model nothing to lean on, and it fabricates confidently."}
{"conclusion": "n_31e625840e", "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "arg_ecb7ec81b5", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_65585a03a2", "n_df070e92a2"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - cot-wei.md", "message": "### 3.2 Results", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Learning System/Lessons/Lesson — prompt-engineering — 2026-10-28.md", "message": "Pollution trace produced at first press", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "Chain-of-thought gains appear only at ~100B+ parameters while smaller models emit fluent but illogical chains, and every sampled token is fed back as the next input, so an illogical step becomes the conditioning context for all remaining steps: the error compounds instead of being corrected, and one-shot answering wins below that scale."}
{"conclusion": "n_246ac52e58", "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "arg_a064333351", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_31e625840e", "n_65585a03a2"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "How the learner reconciled it: 10–40% scoped to models greater than ~100B", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "## Key Terms — 10-40%", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "If the chain-of-thought gain exists only at ~100B+ parameters and is a loss below that line because the chain pollutes its own context, then the quoted 10–40% can only be the large-model regime average, and the same number stated without its scale precondition is false at the small-model end."}
{"conclusion": "n_d51c37fce9", "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "arg_284cfc80d9", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_113f51dedb"], "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Lessons/Lesson — prompt-engineering — 2026-10-28.md", "message": "Graduation — entailment check, one-off link completion (pass)", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "The fixed case set exists so that a change which fixes one case while regressing another is visible; a single one-off judgment has no prior version to regress against, so there is no comparison to anchor and the held expected-output set is not required."}
{"conclusion": "n_00da07a1c2", "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "arg_376514a455", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_a54e93e026"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "Killed detour — concentration measures how hard the model was squeezed, not whether the output is right", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "If the role is so specific that few training examples match, the model will hallucinate", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "An over-specific role leaves the probability mass concentrated on few, likely-wrong arms and the model fills the thin slice with confident fabrication, so a peaked distribution is compatible with a wrong answer and concentration cannot function as a score of quality."}
{"conclusion": "n_0798381606", "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "arg_fc76215b4b", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_2d3562299b", "n_4f38dd58d1"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Non-Functional (requirements label).md", "message": "The two readings", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "A non-functional requirement states how well the system behaves while running ∧ \"nothing is functioning\" names a system state while \"non-functional\" names a requirement class ⟹ an unmet non-functional requirement produces a system that runs badly, not one that never runs."}
{"conclusion": "n_e40fb3a681", "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "arg_4fda8777db", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_0798381606", "n_4f38dd58d1"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Non-Functional (requirements label).md", "message": "Why the misreading matters", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "An unmet non-functional requirement leaves a system running but behaving badly ∧ the label names a requirement class rather than a system state ⟹ reading the label as \"broken\" recasts a missed quality target as an outage and hides that the requirement was never met."}
{"conclusion": "n_79ed0217b1", "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "arg_2e7ffb40a3", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_631d0704d1", "n_f31f8dd1dd"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## Why the rule cannot be the decider — three breakdowns", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "The legal set is the set of tokens whose transition is defined from the parser state (which carries string-open, expectation, required-keys-left and literal progress) ∧ the teaching rule returns a category list keyed only on the final character plus parse success ⟹ wherever two different parser states share a final character (a value that just closed, a literal in progress, a parseable prefix with required keys missing) the rule returns the wrong legal set, so a parseable prefix is not a finished schema instance."}
{"conclusion": "n_d14bda837f", "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "arg_a6070dc345", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_c4a9f0454f", "n_d1ef256798", "n_f31f8dd1dd"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## The mechanism: mask, then renormalise", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - xgrammar.md", "message": "Their logits are set to − ∞ , effectively assigning them zero probability after the softmax operation and preserving the relative probabilities of other valid tokens.", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "The decoder applies the parser's legal set as a mask that sends rejected tokens' logits to −∞ ∧ softmax is exp(z_i)/Σ_j exp(z_j) and exponentiating makes each term positive ⟹ a masked token contributes exp(−∞) = 0 to the numerator, so its probability is exactly zero while the survivors renormalise and keep their relative ordering."}
{"conclusion": "n_fc7be9212a", "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "arg_6dcbcb1187", "op": "assert", "premises": ["n_1f407a07c9", "n_7897492cfd"], "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Structured Outputs (JSON contract).md", "message": "The one-line version: **enforcement tiers catch grammar failures, not semantic ones.**", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "A grammar defines which strings over an alphabet are valid ∧ the correctness of an extracted value is a relation between the output and the source document, not a property of the string ⟹ anything that enforces a grammar (schema mode, masking, a validator) can only reject strings, so it catches grammar failures and is silent about a hallucinated value."}
